  message: "EXPLICITLY NOT COVERED BY THE 2026-08-17 PERF CLOSE. The owner closed the\nprocessing-EFFICIENCY stream; this is a CORRECTNESS item that happened to be\nfound while measuring.\n\nduration_ms is serialized provenance published to every consumer of a survey, and\nunder a ThreadPoolExecutor it reports per-pass WALL time — so it overlaps AND\ninflates with contention. The same 43 bash files billed 2.3s or 49.9s depending\nonly on what the python analyzer was doing alongside. A reader takes it for cost\nand concludes an analyzer is pathologically slow; I did exactly that before\nrunning the control. That defect is unchanged by our decision to stop optimizing,\nand it will mislead the NEXT person to measure — including whoever re-opens a\nparked perf item on the strength of a card sum.\n\nStays todo_hard at P2."  # da63
- op: discuss  # 5b71
  at: '2026-10-01T00:58:29Z'  # 5b71
  by: agent  # 5b71
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 5b71
  clock: 3  # 5b71
  nonce: 5b71  # 5b71
  message: "PREMISE CHECK 2026-09-30 (dev 7f0f993843): CURRENT-WITH-UPDATES. The mechanism holds as written. The \"schema doc\" option is already half true, and the same defect applies to linkers.\n- analyze/all_analyzers.py:410 still dispatches analyzers through ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=min(len(analyzers), cpu_count)). Per-pass timing is wall time measured inside the worker thread: analyze/base.py:4336 and :4506, `int((time.time() - start_time) * 1000)`. That is wall time, and time.time() is also non-monotonic. No process_time/thread_time/cpu_ms exists anywhere in packages/*/src (grep: 0 hits). ir.py:366 `duration_ms: int = 0` has no comment saying what it measures.\n- ANALOGUE NOT IN THE ROW: linkers/registry.py runs each priority cohort in a ThreadPoolExecutor (docstring :36-38). Linker duration_ms is perf_counter wall around the body (:716/:730-733), so linker cards inflate the same way. 86 src files assign duration_ms.\n- UPDATE TO OPTION (b): the schema already labels it. docs/schema.json duration_ms = \"Wall-clock duration in milliseconds\", from scripts/generate_schema_lib.py:907, which dates to a9d403a560 (2026-06-10, before this row). The spec also calls it wall-clock (hypergumbo-spec.md:567). So \"rename/document as wall\" is partly in place. The defect that remains is the one the title states: no cost field, and nothing near the field says it overlaps and inflates under a pool.\n- INV-gizik's duration_ms>0 rule (ir.py:378, :466-472) is unaffected by any of (a)-(c).\n- NOT RUN: the contention measurement (no survey). The 2.3s vs 49.9s figures are the row's own and were not re-measured.\nPROPOSED: extend the scope to linker passes (registry.py pool). Note that the schema wording already exists, so option (b) becomes \"add the overlap/contention caveat plus a sibling cost field\" rather than a rename."  # 5b71
- op: update  # f8a0
  at: '2026-10-01T01:36:06Z'  # f8a0
  by: agent  # f8a0
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f8a0
  clock: 4  # f8a0
  nonce: f8a0  # f8a0
  set:  # f8a0
    description: "MEASURED 2026-08-17. Passes are dispatched through ThreadPoolExecutors, and each pass's\nduration_ms is its own WALL time. This applies to analyzers (analyze/all_analyzers.py ~410,\ntimed with time.time() in analyze/base.py ~4336/~4506) and to linkers (linkers/registry.py\nrun_all_linkers runs each priority cohort in a pool, timed with perf_counter ~716/~730). Two\nconsequences, both of which have already misled decisions on this tracker.\n\n1. CARD SUMS DO NOT SUM TO WALL. 96 cards total 157.7s inside a 93.9s wall span. Any \"share\n   of wall\" computed from card sums is inflated. That arithmetic produced the 22%/7%/71%\n   analyzer/linker/overhead split recorded on WI-balaf and WI-kazas, which direct phase\n   timing later contradicted.\n\n2. WORSE: A CARD INFLATES WITH CONTENTION, so it is not even a stable measure of the pass's\n   own cost. Two fresh-process runs, same repo, same analyzer, SAME 43 FILES both times:\n\n       max_files=1   bash card =  2.3s   (python analyzing 1 file alongside)\n       uncapped      bash card = 49.9s   (python analyzing 1121 files alongside)\n\n   Bash did identical work and its number moved 21x. It was waiting on the GIL while the\n   python analyzer ran 63s of CPU-bound work in a sibling thread. A reader who takes\n   duration_ms as \"what this pass cost\" concludes that the bash analyzer is pathologically\n   slow at 1.15 s/file; it is not. The same holds for linker cards within a priority cohort\n   (not separately measured).\n\nWHY THIS IS A DEFECT AND NOT A DOC GAP: duration_ms is SERIALIZED provenance (ir.py\nAnalysisRun.duration_ms ~366 -> to_dict), so it is published to every consumer of a survey,\nand INV-gizik already reasons about it (\"a pass that emitted output cannot serialize\nduration_ms=0\"). The schema already labels it \"Wall-clock duration in milliseconds\"\n(scripts/generate_schema_lib.py ~907, since 2026-06-10), and the spec calls it wall-clock.\nWhat is missing is (i) any statement that cards overlap and inflate under a pool, and (ii)\nany field that measures the pass's own cost. No process_time/thread_time/cpu field exists\nanywhere in packages/*/src.\n\nOPTIONS, not yet decided:\n  a) Record CPU time (time.thread_time_ns / process_time per worker) alongside or instead of\n     wall, so the field measures the pass rather than the machine's load.\n  b) Keep wall, add the overlap/contention caveat to the schema description and the\n     AnalysisRun field comment, and add a sibling field for cost.\n  c) Record both, which is what would actually let a reader see contention.\n\nWhatever is chosen, it covers analyzer AND linker passes. The axis-declaration and schema-doc\nsurfaces need updating with it, and scripts/measure-survey-phase-split.py stays the\ninstrument of record for phase cost (it times the phase FUNCTIONS with inclusive/exclusive\naccounting and reconciles to the wall clock).\n\nRevised 2026-09-30 from the premise check (see discussion)."  # f8a0
- op: discuss  # 124f
  at: '2026-10-02T01:49:54Z'  # 124f
  by: agent  # 124f
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 124f
  clock: 5  # 124f
  nonce: 124f  # 124f
  message: "IMPLEMENTED on local branch jgstern-agent/fix/nuvam (unmerged, no PR): 9d75687f03 + 7ee054624d. Option (c): both fields.\n\nWHAT: AnalysisRun gains cpu_ms (Optional[int], always serialized; null = not measured; a measured pass is >= 1, rounded up) = CPU of the thread that ran the pass (time.thread_time_ns), read ON that thread by the new hypergumbo_core/pass_clock.py PassClock, which refuses to be read from another thread. Stamped at: the analyzer pool (it now submits _run_costed, the cost goes to collect_analyzer_result and is stamped before to_dict, covering the WI-didag synthesized run too); the linker chokepoint _run_linker_with_cache (serial and parallel branches); and the six serial synthesis passes (file-symbol synthesis, producer merge, route-materializer, django-cbv-method-expander, enclosure, boundary). At every one of those sites duration_ms now comes from the SAME clock, replacing any figure a pass body timed itself: a body timer covers only part of the call, and the first live run showed the python card at duration_ms=663 beside cpu_ms=1086, which no single span can produce. route-materializer and django-cbv-method-expander were never timed before (their duration_ms was only the INV-gizik 1 ms floor). The schema description, the ir.py field comments and the spec now say duration_ms overlaps and inflates under the pools. docs/schema.json regenerated; SCHEMA_VERSION NOT bumped (left to the integrator).\n\nTESTS: new packages/hypergumbo-core/tests/test_pass_clock.py (14 tests). Mutation control, run through the board: no-op the analyzer stamp, no-op the linker stamp, drop the ceil/floor -> exactly the 6 expected tests red, 7 controls green; restored tree verified byte-identical to the pre-mutation diff. test_linker_registry test_preserves_self_timed_duration pinned the old keep-the-body-figure guard and is replaced. Final targeted smart-test on HEAD: 26695 passed, 0 failed, 4 skipped, 14 xfailed; coverage 100% on changed files. (The run on the first commit had 1 failure, test_autopr_signal_termination::test_a_hangup_is_named_too, a 30 s timeout under load = known flake WI-narum; not in the HEAD run.)\n\nLIVE (fresh process per arm, run_all_analyzers on a git-archive snapshot of origin/dev, 1520 py / 44 bash files):\n  max_files=1: phase wall 6.47 s; duration_ms sum 21.82 s; cpu_ms sum 5.44 s vs process CPU 5.44 s. bash 44 files: duration 3796 ms, cpu 1206 ms.\n  uncapped:    phase wall 135.67 s; duration_ms sum 157.65 s; cpu_ms sum 103.94 s vs process CPU 104.14 s. bash 44 files: duration 4954 ms, cpu 1661 ms. python: duration 132418, cpu 96541.\nFull survey of microservices-demo: 87 cards, 0 without an integer cpu_ms, 0 with cpu_ms > duration_ms+1; bash card 1319 ms wall vs 70 ms CPU.\n\nWHAT THE NUMBERS DO NOT SUPPORT: the row's 21x bash inflation (2.3 s vs 49.9 s) did NOT reproduce on today's dev: bash duration moved 1.30x between the arms. cpu_ms is not load-invariant either: bash cpu_ms moved 1.38x for the same 44 files on a shared, loaded box (thread CPU time rises with frequency scaling / SMT sharing; not separated here). What IS shown: cpu_ms sums reconcile to process CPU within 0.2% in both arms while duration_ms sums do not reconcile to anything. cpu_ms excludes child-process CPU (scip-python / rust-analyzer indexers) and threads a pass starts itself.\n\nNOT CHANGED: the ~90 pass bodies that still self-time with time.time() (their figure is now overwritten at the survey chokepoints); analyze/registry.run_analyzer/run_all_analyzers (no production caller; cpu_ms stays null there); the measurement scripts (filed WI-tuhan-razuv-hitot-gavim-lorof-rohos-vujaz-mapul, P3). No append moved out of its if-guard (WI-tivif's work; integration note left there)."  # 124f
